## 1. Introduction
Given the fast development of heavy industry and the growing population worldwide, human life largely depends on fossil fuels which will be shortage after 2040.[1,2] High demand and abused uses of fossil fuels have in turn led to the severe environmental issues attributed to the abundant emission of carbon dioxide $\mathrm{(CO}_{2}).^{[3]}\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , the main gaseous component, along with many other greenhouse gases, is in the ascendancy in modern life. Owing to the intensive industrial activities, concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the atmosphere has been increased.[3] It is such a continuously increasing amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ that global warming each) are formed as equivalent as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ input (six molecules). This photosynthesis process not only reintroduces the breathable oxygen but also inspires researchers in imitating the natural photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into clean-burning fuels. The reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ using catalysts activated by sunlight, the universal photon energy from Nature, termed photocatalysis is of great importance for renewable energy.
In 1972, Fujishima and Honda discovered the application of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as a semiconductor for water hydrolysis under ultraviolet irradiation.[11] This work has influentially underpinned scientists to artificially synthesize many kinds of photocatalytic materials for the transformation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under ultraviolet or visible light irradiation, of which the latter is more applicable and important due to its environmental friendly attributes.[12–14] To date, semiconductors commonly used for photocatalysis are $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , metal alloys, nanowires, quantum dots, graphenebased composites, and to name but a few.[15–17] Among them, porous crystalline materials have been proven to be promising candidates for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction into high value-added chemicals because of their large internal surface area and densely catalytically active sites.[18,19] Particularly, reticular chemistry of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) possessing endless possibilities to precisely control crystal structures for diverse topologies and chemical features offers great ­opportunities to address challenges encountered in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction (e.g., poor selectivity, low production rate, many byproducts occurred).[4,19]
In other words, MOFs adopting thousands of topologies with unique structural aspects (e.g., large surface area, high conduction, huge accessibility of pore structures, and multiple catalytic sites)[20] in combination with which their structural customization have been targeted by functionalization of organic linkers and/or metal-based clusters by either through predesigned approach or postsynthetic modification (PSM),[21] are capable of catalyzing various organic transformations.[22–24] Particularly, MOF catalysts have been highly effective for the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction owing to their intriguing features that are the 1) effectively adsorption/desorption equilibrium process of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ thanks to the influential Lewis acid sites and large pore volume of MOFs,[25] 2) high charge-carrier mobility originating from highly conjugated frameworks,[14] and (3) catalytically active metal sites (i.e., high value-added compounds can be reduced under a variety of redox potentials).[19]
Studies over the last decade have been sought to investigate the possibility of structural engineering/functionalization to enhance the catalytic activity of MOFs. Notwithstanding vast applicability of MOF catalysts in promoting the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction under visible light,[19,26] challenges in this field remain; studies that underline 1) interplay between MOF structures and their properties for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , 2) welldefined mechanism of the reaction, 3) comprehensive contribution to better-designed systems, and 4) the efficient yield and selectivity of desired products in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photocatalytic reduction are significantly encouraged. In order to fulfill these lofty tasks, this article aims to summarize the fundamental photochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, catalyzed by reticular materials, in which MOFs are mainly targeted. Additionally, we wish to take this chance to discuss the current status of using covalent organic frameworks (COFs)[27–29]—the newly emerged porous and crystalline frameworks whose structures are solely made up of the organic building blocks— for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ although their catalytic uses in this purpose are at the early stage of development and have not been considerably employed yet.[30,31] This work describes the foundational knowledge of the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, including the background, general mechanism and pathways, methods of characterization supporting the reaction mechanism. We aim to shape and consolidate the fundamental of the photoreduction, which is the preliminary interpretability for the second part, applications of reticular materials for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , to supportably introduce the photocatalytic chemistry to emerging scholars. The next goal is to comprehensively discuss achievements of selective MOFs/COFs as advanced photocatalysts for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . This section is structurally cataloged based on the specific features of the reticular-material photocatalysts. Finally, we highlight the challenges and research directions to cast some light on the future perspectives in this field.
## 2. Background of the Photocatalytic $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
The progress of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction is commonly accepted with the following steps.[12,16] First, 1) $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules are adsorbed and interact with a catalyst through physisorption or chemisorption. 2) The required energy will subsequently be supplied by the illumination of the UV or visible light to generate photoexcited electrons (e−) and holes $(\mathrm{h^{+}})$ . In the second step, photogenerated electrons migrate from valence band (VB) to conduction band (CB) while holes remain in the VB. The gap between VB and CB corresponds to the bandgap energy, also termed the energy gap, of a photocatalyst. Next, 3) the photoexcited electrons are disseminated to the catalytically active centers thanks to the high conjugation of the structural framework, in which $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules are permissible to gain electrons to reduce into carbon-neutral fuels. The metal centers either serve as active sites or mediated environment (i.e., mediators that facilitate the move of the photogenerated electrons to other active metal sites). In this step, water is oxidized by reacting with holes in parallel with the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The last step 4) is the recombination of the photoexcited electrons and holes suppressing the reduction process (Figure 1). This step can occur during step (2) and (3) hindering the performance of photocatalysts (e.g., yield, selectivity).
It is important to clarify the effects that influentially impact on the photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . First of all, a photocatalytic system can only absorb the light energy if the total bandgap of the light is equal to or greater than the photocatalyst’s bandgap energy. This energy gap is one of the essential factors in photocatalysis.[16,32] Particularly, the bandgap energy dictates the specific reaction in the multiple proton and electron transfer (MPET) process of the photochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. In order to spontaneously drive the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, the band-edge of CB must be more negative than the reduction potential of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . It should be noted that every single reduction potential of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is different from each other, which will be discussed below. The photo­excited electrons is promoted by photosensitizers, which can ­physically be added into the photocatalytic systems. In reticular chemistry, chemists have advantages at their disposal of the ­structural functionalization—if not at the molecular level—to architecturally functionalize the predesigned structures of MOFs/COFs[26,33] with highly conjugated entities that mainly act as photosensitizers, therefore requiring no additive.
Second of all, the structural frameworks of photocatalysts affect the migration speed of the photoexcited electrons mobilizing from CB region to the active metal centers for the subsequent reaction; an electric field, generated by the photocatalyst structure with high conjugation, pulls these photoexcited electrons. This behavior is so-called “electron mobility.” Therefore, an interplay between the framework and the charge-carrier mobility determines the structural design and catalytic performance of MOF photocatalysts.[34] Based on this relationship, scientists have modified the skeleton of semiconductors to enhance the charge-carrier mobility for the photocatalytic enhancement. Moreover, the recombination of the photogenerated electrons and holes occurring in step (2) and (3) results in suppressing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. The more straightforward the photoexcited electrons and holes are prone to combine, the faster photoreduction will be terminated. Therefore, engineering the crystal structures of photocatalysts to facilitate the long-lived excited electrons has been one of the most commonly investigated topics in the photocatalytic chemistry.[35]
Third of all, we discuss on the redox potentials and kinetics in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. As proposed by Bockris and coworkers[36] widely accepted by scientists in this field, the first reaction that occurred in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction belongs to the formation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{\mathrm{\cdot}}$ which is required a very negative potential at $-1.90{\mathrm{V}}$ versus normal hydrogen electrode (NHE), as presented in Equation (1).[37] This is understandable because of the inert $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ bond in the linear $scriptstyle\mathrm{O=C=O}$ molecule demanding very high dissociation energy of ${>}805\mathrm{~k}]$ mol−1.[2,38,39] The highly kinetic barriers required for the transformation of the symmetrically linear $D\infty_{h}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule to the bent radical anion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{\mathrm{^{-}}}$ adopting $C_{2\nu}$ symmetry imply that this intermediate reaction cannot be catalyzed by semiconductors unless there exists a catalyst possessing a reduction potential more negative than $-1.90{\mathrm{~V}}$ versus NHE. Figure 2 underlines the common reduction potentials of semiconductors. These reduction potentials are less negative than the CB band-edge values of common semiconductors which means that the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction can proceed through MPET, in which $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ receives 2, 4, 6, and $8~\mathrm{e}^{-}$ to favorably reduce into formate or CO, aldehyde, methanol, and methane, respectively (Equations  (2–6)).[19,40] The reduced products mainly depends on the reaction mechanism; and it remains to be unraveled. Additionally, the reduction potentials required for reducing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into various carbon-based products are competitively close to each other, in combination with the high kinetic barriers of reaction, the mixture of products has commonly been observed, and thus lowering the selectivity of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[14] For example, hydrogen production is competitively formed along with MPET products of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction (Equation  (7)). Therefore, reticular materials whose pores and surface structures can simultaneously be engineered hold promise not only to improve the activation pathway of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbate but also to enhance the chargecarrier mobility. As a result, highly efficient reticular-material catalysts for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction are straightforwardly designed and synthesized.
