## 3. Mechanism of the Photocatalytic $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
Owing to the high kinetic barriers, caused by the symmetrical transformation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{\mathrm{\cdot}}$ , the one-electron reduction way is unfavorable.[16] $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, however, in the solution at step (1) of the reduction process, interact with the catalytic surface to generate several adsorption fashions.[14,41] This step containing both adsorption and interaction is well acknowledged as a crucial initiation of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, mainly dictating the reaction mechanism.[14] Particularly, the process that either O atom(s) of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule can coordinate to the metal surface of semiconductors (or metal clusters of MOFs) through the unpaired electron(s) or C atom can gain electron(s) from Lewis basic centers of catalysts (metal-based moieties) generates the bending partial charge species of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{\delta-}$ . With respect to the kinds of coordinate modes between $\mathsf{C O}_{2}^{\delta-}$ and metalbased moieties, which monodentate, bidentate, and a mixed fashion can be observed, there are three kinds of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{\delta-}$ interaction modes, including oxygen coordination, carbon coordination, and mixed coordination.[42,43] The activation energy of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, therefore, has kinetically been lowered and more favorable to the subsequent reactions. Masel and co-workers employed this reactive propensity to introduce 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium tetra-fluoroborate (EMIM- ${\cdot\mathrm{BF}_{4}})$ into the reaction solvent, in which $\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{\delta-}$ species coordinate to EMIM- ${\mathrm{.BF}}_{4}$ to form EMIM- ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{BF}}_{4}$ intermediate, thus decreasing the overpotential of the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO.[44]
The reaction pathway of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction is complicated and accomplished by many elementary steps initiating from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption/activation process to partial charge species of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}^{\delta^{-}}$ formation followed by the electron, proton, or hydrogen radical transformation resulting in the $\mathtt{C-O}$ bond-breaking, $\mathrm{C-H}$ bond-forming associated with deoxygenation and/or hydrogenation processes.[14] Behavior of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -based semiconductors has been dedicatedly studied using both experimental and theoretical methods to understand reaction mechanisms of the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[15,45,46] The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction mechanisms include formaldehyde and carbene pathway. It should be noted that the well-elucidated mechanisms of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction remain unclear. For example, some radical intermediates have not been experimentally detected.[47] In the formaldehyde pathway, the MPET process is dominated, and formaldehyde and methanol are both intermediates, not byproducts. Therefore the efficient photocatalysts will have to adopt the features that stabilize formaldehyde and methanol intermediates to finally produce methane as an 8-electron reduction product. In contrast to this pathway, methanol will be a byproduct in the carbene pathway and no formaldehyde will be observed. Additionally, CO in the carbene pathway may act as a main or byproduct depending on the binding mode of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with the catalyst surface.[14] This means a mixture of CO and methanol or CO and methane will likely be obtained.[48,49] To avoid any overlap between this article and others, we would like to direct interested readers to those cited. $[14,15,47]$ The mechanisms of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction were largely studied based on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ semiconductor and appropriately applicable for other photocatalysts. The mechanism studies, however, conducted on reticular materials possessing well-defined structures, especially MOFs, are highly encouraged to gain a deep insight into the chemistry of photocatalysis using advanced hybrid functional materials.
## 4. Analytical Characterizations for the Photocatalytic $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
Along with the structural characterization of catalysts, methods that are used to analyze the progress of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction to understand the underlying mechanism are important. To this end, it is imperative to use both experimental characterizations and simulations. For the experimental methods, nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR), ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS), electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR), in situ diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS), photoluminescence spectroscopy (PL), and ultrafast spectroscopy have been commonly used, whereas first-principle simulations and density functional theory calculations (DFT) are theoretically utilized. This section aims to provide a useful overview of characterizations for analyzing/studying the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction progress.
NMR spectroscopy has been an irreplaceable measurement used not to detect the products only but unequivocally to determine the stability of MOF catalysts also.[50] Although the ­stability of MOF catalysts can be proven by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis, it is lack of evidence showing the partially structural decomposition of catalysts since the PXRD pattern is diffracted from the crystalline domain in a specific sample. It is understood that hybridity of MOFs composed of organic and inorganic constituents might vulnerably partially be degraded during irradiation leading to the formation of carbon-based products.[2] Therefore, the use of NMR will be beneficial for detecting whether the products come from the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or MOF degradation. To this end, ${}^{12}\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ were used as starting materials, respectively, of which the later gave rise to the formation of carbon-neutral fuels as proven by $^{13}\mathrm{{C}}$ NMR spectroscopy in contrast to the former (i.e., $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectroscopy would not detect any trace of products when the reactant was $^{12}\mathrm{CO}_{2})$ . Nevertheless, NMR cannot provide any information about the crystallinity of MOF photocatalysts. In order to fully determine the stability and durability of MOF photocatalysts, it is required to use NMR and PXRD measurements, if not other supported methods (e.g., scanning electron microscope, nitrogen isotherms, or transmission electron microscope).
In terms of the photocatalytic properties, it is necessary to calculate the bandgap energy of catalysts using UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectroscopy (DRS) as well presented in the literature.[51] Although UV–vis DRS provides the bandgap energy of MOFs, it is not capable of determining the energy of molecular orbitals which will presents more useful information of MOF electronic structures. To this end, UPS measurements can be used.[52,53] Indeed, spectra of the kinetic energy of photogenerated electrons can be obtained, thus elucidating the energy of molecular orbitals during the excitation (i.e., photoelectrons have been promoted to the CB). Based on this information, in combination with UV–vis DRS, the VB and CB maximum energies have been calculated, thus providing the catalytic capability in catalyzing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction of the catalysts. It should be noted that the Mott-Schottky measurement is another alternative for band-edge estimation.[54] This method, however, falls short of providing electronic structures of MOFs.
There are several techniques that we resort to gain a deeper understanding of the photoreduction mechanism. First, the lone pair electrons of radicals and intermediates that occurred in the aldehyde or carbene pathway have been sensitively detected by EPR spectroscopy,[55] which is the characterized method applied magnetic field to study compounds with unpaired electrons, thus proposing the appropriate mechanism. Moreover, EPR is experimentally suitable to measurements that study the transformation of the oxidation state in the metal-oxo clusters of MOFs (e.g., Ti-, Zr-MOFs).[34,56,57] This further reveals the role of active metal sites in the photoreaction. Especially, EPR can be set up and measured in situ to examine the change of valency of metal ions and catalytic active centers during the photocatalytic reaction. In other words, the in situ EPR measurements contribute to identifying the ongoing redox progress.
Another method for oxidation state determination of agents in solution or gaseous medium is in situ DRIFTS.[58–60] This characterization has been commonly established for monitoring the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to methanol or methane. Particularly, Yang and Lin reported the application of DRIFTS studies for investigating the adsorption fashion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ on the surface of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\alpha\mathrm{-Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ core–shell on RGO ( $\operatorname{RGO}{}=$ reduced graphene oxide). Notably, DRIFTS also reveals the oxidation state of ethanol intermediates in the oxidation reaction of ethanol to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ catalyzed by ${\mathrm{V}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{5}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ .[60] The applicability of in situ DRIFTS is wide-ranging and suitable for various photocatalytic reactions with different temperatures.
Finally, to determine if the recombination of photoexcited electrons and holes can be suppressed, researchers use
PL ­spectroscopy.[61–63] This characterization is very useful for the lifetime estimation of the photogenerated electrons and the trap-state emission behavior,[64] normally centered at ${>}650\ \mathrm{nm}$ in the PL spectrum. Furthermore, the exemplified ultrafast spectroscopy measurements will firmly unveil the characteristics of the photoexcited electrons and holes and charge-­separated state, in combination with the lifetime of the ­photogenerated electrons, thus leading to a better insight of the ­photocatalytic mechanism.
The mechanism of the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can be valuably underpinned using the first-principle simulations and DFT methods.[65] These calculations cast light on the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and activation correlated to the active centers in MOF structures and the free-energy of intermediates. Moreover, the efficiency of the highly conjugated backbone in MOFs facilitating the charge-carrier mobility can be fully elaborated based on DFT calculations via the density of state simulations.[66] These simulations offer tremendous opportunities for the predesigned structures of MOF photocatalysts. Nevertheless, it should be noted that optimizations of MOF structures—they often contain huge pore volume with many atoms—at excited states using DFT methods remain challenging, thus limiting the applicability of DFT calculations in predicting the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction mechanism.
## 5. Reticular Materials for the Photocatalytic $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
Based on the overall understanding of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction, the total bandgap energy of reticular materials must be one of the most effective factors affecting the photocatalytic performance. Especially, as discussed in Section  2 and elsewhere,[67–69] the bandgap energy of the catalyst is required to be wider than the redox potential of a specific $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to carbonneutral compound reaction (i.e., the LUMO of semiconductor has to be more negative than the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction potential; and vice versa the HOMO has to be more positive than the corresponding potential of water oxidation reaction). Increasing the CB band-edge of catalyst too negative and far from VB bandedge, however, has led to the fact that the photon energy of irradiated light must be simultaneously increased because electrons require more energy to promote from VB to CB. Conversely, the bandgap energy cannot be too narrow, causing another issue of which the photoexcited electrons might straightforwardly recombine with holes, suppressing the photoreduction.[70] Therefore, it is imperative to appropriately engineer the energy gap of the specific photocatalyst. In MOF chemistry, this approach has been accomplished through the linker functionalization and/or metal-based cluster modification. In addition to this, we discuss the specific features that rely on the interplay between MOF/COF structures and their photocatalytic performance.
## 5.1. Bandgap Engineering
Seifert and coworkers theoretically studied the energetically electronic properties of MOFs using DFT calculations. The authors found that the bandgap of MOFs can be varied from 1.0 to $5.5~\mathrm{eV}$ by linker functionalization[35] using high conjugation systems. It is widely accepted that linkers with either strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction or high electron mobility, affected by functional groups (e.g., amino, naphthyl groups), have been employed for adjusting the visible light absorption of MOFs, namely ligandto-metal charge transfer (LMCT).[71–73]
Li and coworkers reported the seminal work using an aminefunctionalized linker for bandgap engineering of MIL-125 variant.[74] Particularly, the authors employed $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC $\mathrm{\DeltaBDC=}$ benzene dicarboxylate) for synthesizing an isoreticular structure of MIL-125. It should be noted that MIL-125, consisting of 8-membered rings of $\mathrm{Ti_{8}O_{8}(O H)_{4}(-C O_{2})_{12}}$ clusters tethered by BDC moieties, possesses the bandgap energy of $3.6~\mathrm{eV}.^{[56]}$ This bandgap was reduced to be $2.6\mathrm{eV}$ in $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ thanks to the amine-functionalized linker. This result was consistent with the previous report from Xamena and García and coworkers, who, in 2010, published a similar strategy for tailoring the bandgap of UiO-66 applied for hydrogen production.[75] As a result of the amine-based porous structure, $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ remarkably captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with a high capacity of $132.2~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 atm and $273\mathrm{~K~}$ , performing better than the pristine amine-free framework MIL-125 $(98.6\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Interestingly, $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ effectively catalyzed the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formate under the visible light irradiation (with triethanolamine, TEOA, as a sacrificial agent) with a yield of $8.14~\mathrm{\upmumol}$ of ${\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}}$ in the course of $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ , while MIL-125 was inactive for this reaction under the identical conditions.[74] The authors pointed out that the LMCT induced by $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC in the $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ framework (Figure 3) was able to excite the photogenerated electrons which straightforwardly transferred to Ti-oxo clusters and activated the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction through ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ catalytic centers. These ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ active sites were formed by the reduction of $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ atoms (TEOA acted as a donating electron agent for this transformation). The presence of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ in the intervalence charge transfer was proven by EPR measurements bearing a close resemblance to the MIL-125 original work reported by Férey and coworkers in 2009.[56] The authors, however, did not provide data for determining the lifetime of photogenerated electrons.
The amine-functionalized BDC has been identified as an efficient approach for engineering the bandgap of MOFs to further extend the visible light absorption spectra to the red-shift region. Additionally, the metal-oxo clusters were responsible for the energy gap variation. The latter conclusion was firmly proven by Ma and coworkers in 2012, showing the fact that cluster sizes of MOFs contributed to the bandgap tunability.[76] Indeed, the authors analyzed three different sizes of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based clusters ranging from tetrameric structure in IRMOF-9,[77] pentameric structure in $Z\mathrm{n}_{5}$ -BPDC ( $\mathrm{\BPDC=}$ biphenyldicarboxylate),[78] to infinite structure in CPO-7 (Figure  4a).[79] It was found that the bandgaps decreased in the order of IRMOF-9 (3.42  eV), $Z\mathrm{n}_{5}$ -BPDC (3.36  eV), and CPO-7 (3.26  eV). Based on this finding, Li and coworkers tapped into the ability of the structural modification to combine the amine-based linker and active Fe-oxo clusters in a variety of Fe-MOFs ( $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe), $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Fe), and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe)) to produce dual excitation systems being capable of reducing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under the visible light (Figure  4b).[80] Indeed, the authors used the wavelength dependence measurements to study the mechanism of the reaction on the model catalysts of MIL-101(Fe) and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL101(Fe). Remarkably, triangular Fe-oxo clusters were able to absorb the photon energy of the visible light to promote electrons for the transformation of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , making the clusters active for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. This, combined with the LMCT behavior endowed by $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC moieties,[81] verified the dual excitation pathway for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , as evidenced by the increased ${\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}}$ amount when the aminefunctionalized MOFs were used as catalysts. Particularly, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ MIL-101(Fe), $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Fe), and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) promote 178, 46.5, and $30\upmu\mathrm{m}\mathrm{\:HCOO^{-}}$ , respectively, outperforming their amine-free counterparts (59, 29.7, and $9~\upmu\mathrm{m}~\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}$ respectively). The results were consistent with the previous studies reporting the photocatalytic behavior of $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ .[82–84]
Lillerud and coworkers[85] as well as D’Alessandro and coworkers[86] have studied the electronic properties of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(-\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ clusters in UiO-66 based on fcu net which are unable to gain electrons from X-BDC linkers $(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , Br, Cl, F, and H) under the visible light irradiation. To efficiently utilize the photocatalytic features of UiO-66-based structures, chemists modified the organic linkers using either amine functionality or highly conjugated systems, of which $\mathrm{su}$ and Xing presented the latter approach.[87] Particularly, a MOF based on a 2-fold interpenetrated UiO-type structure, denoted by NNU-28, was synthesized using a highly active chromophore linker bearing the anthracene unit. As expected, NNU-28 absorbs the visible light in the red-shift region extending to $650~\mathrm{nm}$ due to the high conjugation of anthracene-based linker adopting high $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction. As a result, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(-\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ clusters in NNU-28 can be activated and effectively catalyze the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . In other words, this is a dual excitation pathway (Figure 5), as proven by in situ EPR spectroscopy showing two characteristic peaks of anthracene-based linker and the intervalence change in the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -oxo cluster, respectively, proceeding in the same way as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Fe-based MOF catalogs.[80] These photocatalytic advancements draw the authors’ attention to employing NNU-28 as a photocatalyst for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-formate reduction. This photoreaction produces a benchmark $\mathrm{\Pi{HCOO^{-}}}$ production rate of $183.3~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}_{\mathrm{MOF}}}^{-1}$ exceeding other Zr-based MOFs used for this purpose. It is accepted that using long linkers with high conjugated configuration for MOF constructions will reduce the energy gap of the photocatalysts and positively affect the photocatalytic activities.[88] Xing’s research demonstrates that reticulation of the elongated linker based on $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -diamino- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -stilbenedicarboxylic acid, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SDCA-NH}_{2}$ , into a variant of UiO-66-type structure, termed $\mathrm{Zr}{\cdot}\mathrm{SDCA}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , displays a broad visible light absorption in the red-shift region, thus efficiently catalyzing the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formate.[89]
## 5.2. Modifications of Active Sites
In an attempt to install a photosensitizer into the MOF framework for employing the catalytically homogeneous characteristics of visible-light sensitive $\mathrm{Re^{\mathrm{I}}}$ -based complex[90] in a porous crystalline system,[91] Lin and coworkers successfully incorporated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}$ and $\mathrm{Re^{I}(C O)}_{3}(\mathrm{dcbpy)Cl}$ (dcbpy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ para$^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $^{.5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{4})$ linkers into UiO-67 (Figure  6).[92] Particularly, the Re-based complex linker $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ was pre-synthesized, and a variant of UiO-67 was subsequently obtained by the reaction of a mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}$ with $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}},}$ termed MOF 4. In this structure, $\mathrm{L}_{4}$ units act not as the visible-light sensitive moieties only but the photoactive sites catalyzing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO reduction also. As a result, in the presence of triethylamine (TEA) acting as a sacrificial agent, MOF 4 photo­ catalyzed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with a turnover number (TON) of 10.9 and 2.5, respectively; the photocatalytic reaction occurred in acetonitrile for $20\mathrm{h}$ . The authors did not include data for underpinning the mechanism of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO reduction, but directed readers to the literature. Especially, a postulated mechanism for this reaction may be a unimolecular pathway, in which $[\mathrm{Re^{I}(b p y)}$ $(\mathrm{CO})_{3}(\mathrm{COOH})]$ intermediate was formed. Although this work demonstrates the efficient ability of MOFs as advanced platforms for encapsulation of catalytically active motifs, it remains a shortcoming that was the leaching problem, detaching $43.6\%$ of the Re-based complex out of the structural framework of MOF 4.
Luo and coworkers solved the leaching problem, encountered in MOF 4, using direct synthesis to incorporate $\mathrm{Ir(ppy)}_{2}(\mathrm{Hdcbpy)}$ $\mathrm{(ppy}=2$ -phenylpyridine) linker (H-L) into a Y-based MOF, termed Ir-CP.[93] Only $3\%$ of Ir leached after $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ of the visible light irradiation. The turnover frequency (TOF) of ${\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}}$ with a rate of $118.8\ \mathrm{\\upmumol\g_{MOF}}^{-1}\mathrm{\h}^{-1}$ , much-exceeding $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (16.3 $\upmu\mathrm{mol~\mathrm{~g_{MOF}}^{-1}~\Delta h^{-1})}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 $(26.4~\upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g_{MOF}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ , was observed.
In these studies, the metal complexes (Re, Ir) were incorporated into the periodic MOF frameworks to exploit their catalytic features through metal-to-ligand charge transfer (MLCT), facilitating the electron excitation and further catalyzing the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The complex active moieties served two roles of 1) photosensitizer harvesting energy from the visible light and 2) catalytically active centers promoting the reaction progress.
Another study was reported by Kong and coworkers introducing the Ru complex, termed ${\mathrm{Ru}}({\mathrm{phen}})_{3}$ -based linker (phen $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ phenanthroline), in a 3D structure of Eu-MOF.[94] Unlike Re- and Ir-based MOFs, Eu-MOF with the covalently bound ${\mathrm{Ru}}({\mathrm{phen}})_{3}$ linker was an efficient catalyst for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formate endowed by both the LMCT and MLCT pathways (Figure  7).[95] Particularly, this work was inspired by two notable factors in the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ using MOFs. These factors are (1) the linkers prone to be antenna moieties for light-harvesting, thus promoting the mobility of the excited electrons through LMCT, and (2) the meticulous functionalization of MOFs, facilitating the reduction reaction thanks to their catalytically Lewis acidic sites (Eu centers). The authors first synthesized the metalloligand $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{L}\right)$ containing strong conjugation of pyridine and benzimidazole linkages. Reticulation of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{L}$ with $\mathtt{E u}(\mathrm{III})$ source afforded the formation of $\mathrm{Eu-Ru(phen)}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{M}\mathrm{(}$ OF whose formulation was $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\mathsf{\Delta}\mathsf{\upmu}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]\cdot(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}.}\end{array}$ (2- $\mathrm{FBA})_{2}{\cdot}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{22}]$ $\left(2{\cdot}\mathrm{FBA}=2\right.$ -fluorobenzoate) as determined by single-crystal X-ray diffraction. $\mathrm{Eu-Ru(phen)_{3}\mathrm{\cdot}M O F}$ adopts a 3D structure with 2-fold interpenetrated framework stabilized by the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking. The 6-coordinate SBUs of $[\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\upmu_{2}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}(\mathrm{-COO^{-}})_{6}]$ are tethered together by triangular L organic linkers generating 1D channels with an internal diameter of $16\times$ $31\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . Due to high porosity, large pore volume, and high chargecarrier mobility thanks to the LMCT behavior, $\mathrm{Eu-Ru(phen)}_{3}\cdot$ MOF was an active photocatalyst for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction under the visible light. It was observed that $\mathrm{Eu-Ru(phen)_{3}\mathrm{-}M O F}$ was effectively able to catalyze the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-formate reduction with a benchmark production rate of $321.9\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{mmol}_{\mathrm{MOF}}{}^{-1}$ within $10\mathrm{~h~}$ of illumination, outstanding reported MOF catalysts used under the same conditions. The detailed studies of mechanism were conducted by UPS, PL, and EPR and further confirmed by DFT calculations. Indeed, the photoelectrons with a long-lived time, ranging from 6.1 to 293.6  ns, were promoted to transfer from Ru centers to ligands via MLCT. These photoexcited electrons subsequently transported to the Eu-based clusters endowed by LMCT pathway, in which the intervalence change of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ was taken place, as proven by EPR measurements. The Lewis active $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\mathrm{-Eu}^{2+}$ clusters were mainly responsible for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formate.[94]
The role of metal active centers in MOFs was widely accepted as Lewis acids promoting a variety of organic transformations.[25]
In the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ metal clusters, however, are not active in all cases. For example, the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -oxo cluster in UiO-66 cannot gain the photoelectrons from BDC entities. An alternative solution to deal with this, apart from the amine-functionalized linker exchange, is to introduce another metal center, serving as a mediated environment for the photoelectron transfer, via PSM. This approach was independently reported by Zhang and coworkers[65] and Kang and coworkers[96] in 2015, employing the PSM method to introduce $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ into 12-coordinate $\mathrm{Zr}$ -oxo cluster of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 generating a mixed metal-based $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66(\mathrm{Zr}/\$ Ti) structure (Figure  8). Thanks to the $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}/\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ mediators, formed under the visible light irradiation, $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}/\mathrm{Zr^{3+}}$ became more active for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formate. At the same time, Kang and coworkers also used the PSM to functionalize UiO-66 with monochatecholate-based BDC units, which were then employed for metal chelation with ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Ga}}^{3+}$ , respectively, termed UiO-66-M(III)CAT $(\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Cr} $ or Ga).[97] UiO$66{\cdot}\mathrm{CrCAT}$ was found to be more efficient than the Ga-derivative counterpart in catalyzing the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formate because $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ held the photogenerated electrons longer than ${\mathrm{Ga}}^{3+}$ .
## 5.3. Suppressing the Recombination of Photoexcited Electrons and Holes
Given that the designed MOFs that are based on strong photosensitizers notably boosting the separation of the photogenerated charges have greatly enhanced the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photo­ reduction performance.[98] MOFs with functionalized entities that can suppress the recombination of excited electrons and holes are of paramount and have been drawn considerable attention from reticular community dealing with structural engineering for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. Taking advantage of the natural photosynthesis where porphyrin-like pigments promote the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction, scientists intensively demonstrate the capability of porphyrin-based materials for the photoreduction in both homogeneous[99,100] and heterogeneous catalysis.[101,102] Motivated by this natural feature, in ­combination with intriguing properties of MOFs, Jiang and Ye research groups reported the highly efficient MOF photocatalysts based on porphyrin linkers and $\mathrm{Zr}$ -oxo clusters in catalyzing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction under the visible light.[50,103]
First, Jiang and coworkers[50] reproducibly synthesized, characterized $\mathrm{PCN-}222^{[104]}$ whose structure is based on connections of the octahedral clusters of 8-coordinate $\mathrm{Zr_{6}(O H)_{8}}$ units and square TCPP linkers $\mathrm{(TCPP~=~}$ tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin), and used it as a remarkable photocatalyst for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formate (Figure 9a). PCN-222 was significantly capable of promoting $30~\mathrm{\textmumol}$ formate within $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ of the visible light illumination in the presence of TEOA (electron donor agent) in acetonitrile. The ultrafast transient absorption (TA) and PL spectroscopy were combined to shed some light on the mechanism of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. Indeed, PCN-222 facilitated the long-lived excited electrons $_{(\approx11}$ ns) in the form of the electron trap state endowed by TCPP moieties. The authors concluded that the time needed to detrap electrons from the deep state was long enough to suppress the recombination of the excited electrons and holes, thus mainly corresponding to the exceptional photocatalytic activity of PCN-222.
Second, Ye and coworkers introduced ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ active sites into the porphyrin-based units of MOF-525 through the PSM to form MOF-525-Zn and MOF-525-Co, respectively.[103] The incorporation of metal active centers, especially ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ open sites, has been recognized as a critical factor for enhancement of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake and improvement of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. These two aspects were proven in the case of MOF-525-Co (Figure 9b). Particularly, the unsaturated ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ sites were strongly beneficial for the uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ compared with the pristine material, as proven by in situ FTIR measurements. Moreover, Co entities were prone to act as “trap sites” to notably stimulate the separation of the photogenerated electrons and holes as well as suppress their recombination, thus facilitating the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. The photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over MOF-525-Co was conducted within $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ of the visible light illumination in the presence of TEOA (electron donor agent). CO was formed with a production rate of $200.6~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{g_{MOF}}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and a small trace of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(36.76~\upmu\mathrm{mol~g_{MOF}}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ . EPR measurements and DFT calculations further supported that the directional migration of the excited long-lived electrons, moving from TCPP units to the unsaturated ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ centers, in which the reduction of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ to ${\mathrm{Co^{+}}}$ significantly suppressed the recombination of the excited electrons and holes, mainly corresponded to the high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction performance.
Besides these two seminal studies, very recently, a study from Lin and coworkers.[105] indicates the importance of eclipsed stacking with accessible space $(\approx7.4\mathrm{~\AA})$ between Coporphyrin-based units in ZrPP-1-Co $\mathrm{PP}=$ polyphenolate-metalloporphyrin; Figure  10) in 1) improving the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ interaction with Co active centers and 2) stabilizing the CO-adduct intermediate for achieving the exceptionally photocatalytic results of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. In this study, the stabilization of CO adduct stemming from the high electron density of the Co-PP units was proven experimentally and theoretically.
## 5.4. MOF Composites
It was observed that the linkers and multiple nuclearities of metal clusters effectively enhance the performance of the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ rather than MOFs with single active sites such as ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ in Co-porphyrin-based units or Re/Ir entities which have been commonly introduced into MOF structures by predesigned linkers and/or PSM method. It is rarely seen that MOFs with single metallic clusters are active for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ due to their inefficiency of the visible light responsiveness. Therefore, it is necessary to incorporate MOFs with antenna-reactor complexes to maximize their catalytic performance. In these cooperative systems, MOFs and the antenna-reactor complexes act as co-catalysts and photosensitizers, respectively. In this section, we selectively describe some of the impressive MOF structures that require additional photosensitizers and MOF composites capable of reducing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under the photocatalytic conditions.
Monometallic cluster-based MOF for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , Co-ZIF-9, was first reported by Wang et  al.[106] ZIFs (zeolitic imidazolate frameworks),[107] a subclass of MOFs, are constructed by functionalized imidazole links and divalent metals (e.g., Zn, Co, or Cu), possessing tetrahedral geometry, thus mimicking the natural topologies of zeolites. Co-ZIF-9 comprises of benzimidazole (bIM) and the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ tetrahedral units. Because bIM was unfavorable to the visible-light-driven excitation of the photogenerated electrons, an additional photosensitizer of $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(\mathsf{b p y}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) was required. Co-ZIF-9 acted as a co-catalyst promoting the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction in a mixture of acetonitrile and water $(4/1$ $\mathrm{v/v)}$ in the presence of TEOA acting as the electron sacrificial agent. After $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ of the visible light illumination, $41.8~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ of CO and $29.9~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ were produced. The role of the Co-benzimidazolate framework in Co-ZIF-9 was indirectly studied by control experiments (i.e., the reaction was conducted without Co-ZIF-9 or with Co-MOF-74), indicating that the high affinity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with the structural framework of Cobenzimidazolate entities was mostly responsible for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction.[106]
Very recently, Li and coworkers synthesized three stable and isoreticular structure MOFs based on $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , respectively.[108] A mixed linker system, $^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}$ -nitrilotribenzoic acid (TCA) and 1,2-di(4-pyridyl)ethylene (dpe), reacted with $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ to generate the corresponding MOFs formulated as $[\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{TCA})_{2}(\mathrm{dpe})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]_{n}$ (MOF-Ni), $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{TCA})_{2}(\mathrm{dpe})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]_{n}$ (MOF-Co), and $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(T C A)_{2}(d p e)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{3}]_{{n}}}$ (MOF-Cu), respectively. The three MOFs were highly stable in a broad range of $\mathrm{\pH}$ $\mathrm{(pH}=3\mathrm{-}13$ ) and active for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under the visible light irradiation. Especially, MOF-Ni adopting a low bandgap energy of $1.5~\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ combined with $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ photosensitizer, was exceedingly capable of catalyzing the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO with a high production rate of $371.6\upmu\mathrm{mol}\up g_{\mathrm{MOF}}{}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ $(22.3~\upmu\mathrm{mol})$ within $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ of the visible light illumination. A trace amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ $(0.5~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ , $8.3\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g_{MOF}}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})$ was also obtained.[108] The authors performed the DFT calculations to shed light on this exceptionally catalytic performance of MOF-Ni and the calculations indicated that Ni open centers strengthening the binding affinity toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules stabilized the $\mathsf{C O}^{2-}$ intermediates (Figure 11), thus facilitating the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. The authors, however, did not provide data showing the photocatalytic properties of MOF-Ni without the photosensitizer. Due to the appropriately low bandgap, MOF-Ni should be able to excite the photoelectrons to reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
The deficiency of some MOFs in boosting the charge separation of the photoexcited electrons and holes limits their photocatalytic performance. To diminish this drawback while retaining other intriguing features of MOFs intact, as discussed in Co-ZIF-9[106] and MOF-Ni,[108] MOFs have been employed as co-catalysts that demand the additional photosensitizers. The photosensitizers can be the noble metal complexes,[109] transition-metal-based semiconductors,[110] conjugated polymers,[111] or metal nanoparticles.[112]
To enhance the catalytic performance, including high selectivity and durability of photocatalysts, Yaghi and coworkers demonstrate how the structural flexibility of a UiO-67 variant can be fine-tuned to fulfill the above requirements.[113] Notably, the high photosensitizer-based organic linker of $\mathrm{Re^{I}(C O)_{3}(b p y d c)(C l)}$ , denoted by RTC, was synthesized; the variant of UiO-67 structure was produced by the reaction of a mixture of RTC and BPDC with ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ source (Figure  12a). The number of active centers in the resultant material, termed ${\mathrm{Re}}_{n}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ $[\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}=\boldsymbol{0}$ , 1, 2, 3, 5, 11, 16, and 24; n was counted per the primitive cell of MOF structure) bearing the effectively photo­ catalytic functionality, was precisely controlled. The authors experimentally recognized that ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}–{\mathrm{MOF}}$ performed the highest catalytic activity in the selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO photoreduction producing a CO-TON of $0.14\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . Interestingly, ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}{\mathrm{-MOF}}$ was placed on silver nanocubes (Figure  12b) to form a MOF composite, namely $\mathrm{Ag{\subset}R e_{3}{\cdot}M O F{\cdot}16\ n m}$ whose photocatalytic performance was found to be optimum with 7-fold enhancement compared to the pristine ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ . This phenomenon was ascribed to the plasmonic behavior of the synergistic system originating from ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}{\mathrm{-MOF}}$ placing onto the surface of Ag ­nanocubes. The authors especially stated, “the intensified near-field created at the surface of $\operatorname{Ag}$ nanocubes can be absorbed by ReTCs incorporated into the ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ layer for photocatalytic enhancement.”[113] This exceptional catalyst was stable when effectively performing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction up to $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ without any loss of performance, outstanding the molecular complexes that have commonly been lost the activity due to the unavoidable dimerization which is precluded in ${\mathrm{Re}}_{n}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ thanks to the strong covalent bond of the Re centers and the MOF backbone.
Wang and coworkers have recently tapped into the advance of the plasmonic resonance and reported a MOF composite consisting of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-68 and Pt nanoparticles (NPs).[114] The noble NPs have been evaluated their catalytically productive activities in (1) creating the heterojunctions with MOFs which facilitate the electron migration and (2) enhancing the photoexcited charge separation due to the localized surface plasmon resonance (LSPR).[115] To this end, the authors sought to synthesize three kinds of MOF composites: $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathbf{x})@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-68 (Pt NPs were incorporated inside MOF), $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{x})/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -UiO-68 (Pt NPs were loaded outside MOF), and Pt(x)- ${\mathrm{.NH}_{2}}$ -UiO-68 (Pt NPs were both inside and outside MOF) where $\mathbf{x}=1$ , 2, and 4 corresponding to the percentage of Pt NPs loaded. Pt NPs were homogeneously dispersed in the structure of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-68, as proven by high-resolution transmission electron microscopy. All MOF composites were investigated for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under the visible light illumination within $^\mathrm{~6~h~}$ in the presence of TEOA acting as the electron donor agent. The photo­catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pt}(2)\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-68 produced CO as the solely converted product with a production rate of $400.2~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{g_{MOF}}^{-1}$ , outperforming the other two samples.[114] This work highlights the ability of MOF-semiconductor heterojunctions in not only facilitating the electron migration via the NP-MOF interfaces but also enhancing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ­photocatalytic activity because of the plasmonic resonance stemming from NP-MOF composites. This work, in addition to many others, also casts light on using the MOF composites, including metal-transition-based-semiconductor/MOFs,[115–119] quantumdot/MOFs,[120,121] and conjugation-polymer/MOFs[122–125] for enhancing the photo­catalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
To enhance the photoexcited charge separation and further stimulate the migration of these excitons to charge-carrier systems, scientists have developed the Z-scheme photocatalysis[126,127] using the photocatalysts that consist of multicomponents. Most MOF-based composites belong to type-II heterostructures[128] and suffer the fast combination of the photo­excited electrons and holes. Z-scheme MOF-base composites can solve that problem.[68] The Z-scheme has been indicated to effectively enhance the photoexcited charge separation while the potential redox properties are remained intact. Bearing this idea, Liu and coworkers reported a MOF composite heterostructure containing three components of oxygendefective $\mathrm{{znO}}$ , reduced graphene oxide, and UiO- $66\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}}$ , denoted by $\mathrm{O{-}Z n O/r G O/U i O{-}66{\cdot}N H_{2}}$ ; and its photocatalytic property for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction was studied.[129] Indeed, in the course of $6\mathrm{~h~}$ illumination of the visible light, the photo­ reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{O{-}Z n O/r G O/U i O{-}66{\cdot}N H_{2}}$ resulted in a mixture of methanol and formic acid with a production rate of 34.83 and $6.41\upmu{\mathrm{mol}}\upmathrm{g_{M O F}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. The lifetime of the photoexcited electrons of $\mathrm{O{-}Z n O/r G O/U i O{-}66{-}N H_{2}}$ was found to be 5.99  ns which far exceeds the compared counterparts of $_{\mathrm{{O-ZnO}}}$ (1.25 ns) and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (1.71 ns), thus indicating the high effectiveness of this MOF heterojunction in suppressing the fast combination of the photoexcited electrons and holes. The PL measurements also confirmed that the defect $_{\mathrm{{O-ZnO}}}$ constituent significantly corresponded to the enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and stabilized $-\mathrm{OH^{\bullet}}$ radicals, mainly responsive for the methanol formation.[129]
## 5.5. MOFs with Functionalities Enhancing $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Interaction
The reticular community has focused largely on the designed and functionalized structures of MOFs for improving the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake in pre-combustion and selectively separating it from the postcombustion (i.e., flue gas stream).[130,131] The conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to value-added compounds is the next step of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ utilization, and MOFs are one of the widely concerned materials capable of fulfilling this goal. Being correlated with the sequestration, MOFs adopting high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity are mostly concerned to investigate their applicability in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion. This is comprehensible because MOFs that bear tunable frameworks strongly interacting with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ either via open metal sites (OMS) or basic organic linkers are effective for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .[98] There have been many reports of using such active sites that display the high affinity toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules through OMS (Fe, Cu, Al),[80] Co-porphyrin-based units,[132] and amine-functionalized linkers for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .[57,89] Herein, we discuss in detail two exceptional MOFs whose structures present strong interaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (via hydrogen bonds and chemisorption), thus stabilizing intermediates and facilitating the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction progress.
Unlike Co-ZIF-9 bearing the benzimidazolate functional framework lacking the visible-light-driven excitation capability, thus requiring an additional photosensitizer, adenine-based MOFs convert $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into formate without photosensitizers. Particularly, Lan and coworkers synthesized two biomimetic MOFs consisting of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ linked by either adenine (HAD) and butanedioic acid (BA; AD-MOF-1; $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathrm{HAD})_{2}(\mathrm{AD})_{2}.$ (BA)] $\cdot\mathrm{DMF}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ or adenine and isobutyric acid (IA; AD-MOF-2; $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathrm{HAD})_{2}(\mathrm{AD})_{2}(\mathrm{IA})_{2}]\cdot\mathsf{D M F})$ .[133] AD-MOF-1 and AD-MOF-2 are isoreticular structures, in which two ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ atoms in the form of paddle-wheel units are tethered by two carboxylates and two HAD entities. The ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ centers feature the tetrahedral pyramid geometry with five coordination numbers (three from N-based HAD and two from the O-based carboxylate group). It is worth noting that the intriguing structural features of AD-MOF-1 and AD-MOF-2 rely on the free amino groups of N-based aromatic rings, pointing inwards the hexagonal pores of 7.9 Å (Figure  13a). Due to the presence of the densely Lewis basic sites of HAD units in the confined pore structures,[131] AD-MOF-1 and AD-MOF-2 exhibit the high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of 52.15 and $41.75\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Furthermore, the bandgap energy was calculated to be $1.76\ \mathrm{eV}$ for AD-MOF-1 and $1.52\mathrm{eV}$ for AD-MOF-2.[133]
With these two exceptional features of high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity and low energy gaps, AD-MOF-1 and AD-MOF-2 were investigated their photocatalytic activity in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. The experiments were conducted under aqueous acetonitrile solution in the presence of triisopropanolamine (TIPA) as a sacrificial agent. Interestingly, HCOOH was solely found to be the liquid product, and no gaseous products were detected. Indeed, after $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ of the visible light illumination, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photo­reduction using AD-MOF-1 catalyst resulted in $7.16~\mathrm{\upmumol}$ of HCOOH $(179.0~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g_{MOF}}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ . When using AD-MOF-2, $22.16~\mathrm{\\upmumol}$ HCOOH (443.2  mmol $\mathrm{g_{MOF}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ was produced after $^\textrm{\scriptsize5h}$ , performing better than AD-MOF-1 and being one of the benchmarked results reported to date. Insufficient leaching of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ $(<0.065\%)$ and recycling ability of the two MOFs (four consecutive cycles) demonstrate their practical applicability.[133]
The controlled experiments were carried out, proving that it is mandatory to reticulate the HAD linker into the 3D structures of AD-MOF-1 and AD-MOF-2 for the exceptionally catalytic activity. The authors postulated that the photocatalytic performance of AD-MOFs was due to the high crystalline framework with well-defined structures, and the reaction likely proceeded close to adenine-based entities, if not fully coordinated to the organic ligands, instead of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ centers. This hypothesis was then confirmed by the DFT calculations showing the strong interaction (hydrogen bonding) between $o$ -amino groups and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, which mainly corresponded to stabilize the intermediate structures (Figure  13b) and further activate the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. This work showcases another pathway for designing green MOF materials suited to the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction.
It should be noted that the strong interaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with active sites in MOFs via chemisorption leads to a bottleneck of the activation process,[134] in which $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is hardly desorbed for the next step of the reduction progress, thus impeding the catalytic activity of catalysts. The second example that we should draw attention is the report from Yang and coworkers publishing a strategy of creating chemisorption sites in MIL$101\mathrm{-Cr}$ while balancing the desorption of intermediates, thus enhancing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction.[135] This work additionally provides an appealing approach for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction enhancement based on the modification of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption sites.[136] Particularly, the functional MIL-101-Cr with sulfonic groups (MIL-101-Cr- $\mathrm{\Delta}S{\mathrm{O}}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ ) was synthesized in combination with another three MOFs including MIL-101-Cr-EN $\mathrm{EN}=$ ethylenediamine), MIL-101-Cr-DETA (DETA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ diethylenetriamine), and MIL-101-Cr-TETA (TETA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ triethylenetetramine). EN, DETA, and TETA were postsynthetically grafted onto the pore surfaces of the MIL-101-Cr- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ material. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction was conducted under gas-phase conditions using MIL-101-Cr, $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101{\cdot}\mathrm{Cr}{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ and MIL-101-Cr-EN, respectively.[135] CO and methane were the main products. Indeed, CO was selectively produced with $96.5\%$ and a rate of $47.2~\mathrm{\upmumol~g_{MOF}}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1};$ , outperforming MIL-101-Cr and MIL-101-Cr- $\mathrm{\Delta}S{\mathrm{O}}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , therefore demonstrating that the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption stemming from EN entities grafted on MIL-101- $\mathrm{Cr-SO_{3}H}$ surfaces contributed to increasing the performance of the photocatalyst. Moreover, MIL-101-Cr-EN durably performed this interesting activity for at least $40\mathrm{~h~}$ (four consecutive cycles). The high photocatalytic performance of MIL-101-Cr-EN correlated to its $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity (3.57 mmol $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1}}\end{array}$ ) higher than that of MIL-101-Cr $(3.05\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1})}$ and $\mathrm{MIL-101-Cr-SO_{3}H}$ $(2.05\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1})}$ . The authors explained that the incorporation of EN entities not only induced the chemisorption, as proven by high isosteric heat of adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}}=37\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})$ higher than that of MIL-101-Cr$\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $(Q_{\mathrm{st}}=32\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})$ and MIL-101-Cr $(Q_{\mathrm{st}}=18\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})$ but also optimized the pore structures, thus improving the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption. Additionally, the structural modification of MIL101-Cr- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ with EN moieties gave rise to the high optical responsiveness indicated by its optically red-shifted absorption. Interestingly, DETA and TETA-based versions of MIL-101-Cr$\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ were less active in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO photoreduction than
MIL-101-Cr-EN attributed to the low uptake capacity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and high $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ (i.e., the energy needed to desorb intermediates would be higher than using MIL-101-Cr-EN catalyst, thus making the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction unfavorable).[135] This work pave the way for the newly designed MOF systems with appropriate functionalities for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction.
