## 5.6. COFs for the Photocatalytic $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
COFs have been intensively developed for the past decade due to their high order of structurally tunable flexibility, although the structures of COFs lack in topological diversity compared to MOFs, which are due mainly to the limitation of designed SBUs and the problem of crystallization.[137,138] Especially, 2D COFs have been exponentially increased owing to 1) their intriguing structural features of organic, crystalline, and porous frameworks and 2) straightforward syntheses well beneficial for many applications.[139] It is apparent that COFs, however, are in their infancy stage of development and required more studies in increasing the diversity of structures for basic research and applied science.[137,140,141]
Inspired by the high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO photocatalytic activity of Re-based complexes, MOFs bearing the Re complexes in the framework, and the high conjugation of triazine functionality, Zhang and coworkers reported the synthesis of a triazine COF consisting of $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl-5,5-dialdehyde (BPDA) and $^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}$ -(1,3,5-triazine-2,4,6-triyl) trianiline (TTA) units and adopting the hexagonal layers of hcb net (Figure  14).[146] The pyridine functional groups of BPDA moieties allows the PSM to incorporate Re complexes into the catalyst structure. Especially, $\mathrm{Re(CO)}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ entities were precisely controlled and well dispersed in the framework of the triazine-COF (Figure  14). The TA and X-ray transient absorption measurements indicated that the photoexcited electrons were partially located at the regions containing $\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ complexes (photogenerated electrons transferred from COF to ${\mathrm{Re}}({\mathrm{CO}})_{3}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{\AA_{\prime}}^{\prime}$ ), thus stabilizing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adducts during the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. As expected, Re-COF acting as an effective photocatalyst in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction in the presence of TEOA (electron donor agent) generated ${\approx}15$ mmol $\mathrm{g_{Re\mathrm{-}C O F}}^{-1}$ CO. Re-COF photocatalyst durably catalyzes the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO photoreduction for at least $20\mathrm{~h~}$ . CO-TON was found to be 48 and far exceeding the homogeneous $\mathrm{Re(CO)_{5}C l}$ photocatalyst (22-fold enhancement).[146] Only $2\%$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was observed under the optimized conditions demonstrating the excellent selectivity of Re-COF in the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO. The PSM strategy for incorporating $\mathrm{Re(CO)}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ complexes into the framework of COFs was explored more by Cooper and coworkers successfully synthesizing Re-bpy- $\mathrm{\cdotsp}^{2}\mathrm{c}$ -COF based on 1,3,6,8-tetrakis(4-formylphenyl)pyrene (TFPPy) and $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -bis(cyanomethyl)-2,2′-bipyridine units.[147] Re-bpy- $\operatorname{sp}^{2}\operatorname{c}\operatorname{cOF}$ photocatalytically catalyzed the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to form CO with a production rate of $1040~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{g_{COF}{}^{-1}\ h^{-1}}.$ . Its selectivity, however, achieved only $81\%$ over $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ after $17.5\mathrm{h}$ of the visible light illumination. A similar approach was reported by El-Roz and coworkers using Re-TpBpy COF $(\mathrm{Tp}=1,3,5$ -triformylphloroglucinol; $\mathrm{\Bpy=}$ $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -diamine) which catalyzed the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO with a production of 3.25  mmol $\mathrm{g_{COF}}^{-1}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ illumination of the visible light.[148] Recently, Lan and coworkers have reported the approach of employing $\operatorname{sp}^{2}\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{COF}$ to synthesize $\mathrm{sp}^{2}\mathrm{c.COF_{\mathrm{dpy}}}$ consisting of TFPPy donor and $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -([2,2′-bipyridine]- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -diyl)diacetonitrile (BPyDAN) acceptor building units. The $\mathrm{sp}^{2}\mathrm{c.COF_{\mathrm{dpy}}}$ was then metalated with $\mathrm{{MCl}}_{2}$ $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Co, Ni, Cu) to correspondingly produce the $\mathrm{sp^{2}c\mathrm{\mathrm{-}C O F_{\mathrm{dpy}}\mathrm{-}M}}$ photocatalysts. Particularly, $\mathrm{sp}^{2}\mathrm{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{COF}_{\mathrm{dpy}}{\cdot}\mathrm{Co}$ displays the best performance compared with the other catalogs in promoting the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to CO in water with $81.4\%$ selectivity and a production rate of $17.93\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{COF}}-$ 1.[149]
Yu and coworkers synthesized a COF bearing single metal sites highly corresponding to the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO photoreduction.[150] Particularly, the authors used the same TpBpy COF mentioned above to access the PSM using $\mathrm{Ni(ClO_{4})_{2}}$ . The resultant COF, denoted by Ni-TpBpy, combined with a photosensitizer of $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}.$ , effectively catalyzed the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO with a production rate of $4,057\ \mathrm{\upmumol\gcoF}^{-1}$ for $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The photogenerated electrons strongly migrated from $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ to the $\mathrm{Ni}$ active sites. The intense interaction between Ni active centers and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was highly responsible for the formation and stabilization of $\mathrm{L}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adduct $\mathrm{(L}=2,2^{\prime}$ - bpy), thus facilitating the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO photoreduction. This mechanism was confirmed by the DFT calculations.[150] At the same time, Lan group reported COFs based on earth-abundant $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn}$ active centers metalated using PSM method.[151] Especially, metal active sites in DQTP COF-M $(\mathrm{DQ}=2,6$ -diaminoanthraquinone; $\mathrm{TP}\ =\ 2{,}4{,}6$ -triformylphloroglucinol; $\textbf{M}=$ Ni, Co, Zn) were intercalated between two layers of the COF through strong coordination bonds of $\mathrm{C=O-M}$ (strong interaction); metal active sites in DATP COF-M ( $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{DA}}=2{,}6$ -diaminoanthracene) were physically located near the TP moieties (weak interaction). The high charge-carrier mobility originating from the high $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction of linkers contributed to enhancing the photocatalytic properties of the resultant COFs toward the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. The highest photocatalytic performance over CO formation with a rate of $1020\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g_{COF}}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ was observed when using DQTP COF-Co, whereas HCOOH was generated with $90\%$ selectivity over CO when using DQTP COF-Zn.[151]
One of the drawbacks in the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO using COFs as photocatalysts is the low yield. To efficiently increase the yield of CO formation, Jiang group recently has employed nanosheets of COF-367-Co (COF-367-Co NSs) as a heterogeneous photocatalyst for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction.[152] Particularly, the excellent photocatalytic activity of COF367-Co NSs was ascribed to the high interaction between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ­molecules and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ active centers, as widely observed in Coporphyrin-based MOFs. The Co active centers combined with porphyrin units stabilized the $\mathsf{C O O H^{\ast}}$ intermediates and further facilitated the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO reduction with an exceptional production rate of $10,162\ \mathrm{\\upmumol\\upg_{COF}}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ ( $78\%$ selectivity; $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was formed as a byproduct). At the same time, Lan and coworkers reported TTCOF-Zn consisting of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -porphyrin units and 2,3,6,7-tetra(4-formylphenyl)-tetrathiafulvalene (TTF) linkers. This COF displays appropriate band structures significantly inducing the separation of the photoexcited electrons and holes. Additionally, TTCOF-Zn with the charge-carrier mobility acts as the exceptional photocatalyst for the highly selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[153] In particular, TTCOF-Zn based on the high conjugation system, for the first time, catalyzes the photocatalytic conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO with a yield of $12.33~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}$ and $\approx100\%$ selectivity without the additional photosensitizer and sacrificial agent (Figure  15). Very recently, TABB-COF[154] consisting of (5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-aminophenyl)-porphyrin) and 2,5-dibromo-1,4-benzenedialdehyde was reported by Zou and Su et  al.; TABB-COF demonstrated an excellent selectivity of CO production $(95.6\%)$ with a conversion rate of $295.2~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ $\mathrm{g_{COF}}^{-1}$ within $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Similar to TTCOF-Zn, TABB-COF required no sacrificial agents, and the photoreduction reactions in both cases were carried out under aqueous medium showing the excellent durability for at least five cycles.
Based on the exceptional activity of TTCOF-Zn consisting of high conjugation linkers of donor-acceptors, Lan and coworkers have further attempted to synthesize COFs whose SBUs are based on the strong electronic conjugation of 2,3,6, 7,10,11-­hexahydroxytriphenylene (HHTP) and tetrafluorophthalonitrile (TFPN) (COF-316) or 2,3,5,6-tetrafluoro-4-pyridinecarbonitrile (TFPC) (COF-318).[155] With respect to exploiting the Z-scheme strategy in the photocatalysis, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction performance over Z-schematic semiconductor/COF heterojunctions may be improved. Bearing this idea in mind, the authors synthesized a series of COF heterojunctions by combing two COFs (COF-316 and COF-318) and three kinds of semiconductors $(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , ${\tt B i}_{2}\tt{W O}_{6}$ , and $\scriptstyle\alpha\mathrm{-Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})$ ). Among them, the Z-schematic $\mathrm{COF}{-}318{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ photocatalyst exhibited the exceptional activity in catalyzing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO photoreduction. Especially, within the course of $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ of the visible light illumination, without any additives (photosensitizer and electron ­sacrificial agent), $100\%$ selectivity of CO was observed with a production rate of $69.67\upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g_{catalyst}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . This result exceeded the performance of COF-316-SCs, COF-318-SCs, and semiconductors $\mathrm{(TiO}_{2}$ , ${\tt B i}_{2}\tt{W O}_{6}$ , and $\alpha{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3};$ ).[155] The intricate and comprehensive characterizations supported by DFT calculations underpinned the hypothesis of the authors: the covalent binding between COF and semiconductor facilitated the photo­ excited charge separation and further stimulated the photogenerated electrons to straightforwardly transfer to the active catalytic sites to catalyze the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. This work paved the way for designing the Z-schematic photocatalytic systems that are based on MOF or COF heterojunctions.
It is worth noting that COFs may be less active than MOFs in catalyzing the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ because COFs do not bear the active metal clusters; and that is understandable why scientists are prone to metalate COFs. While MOFs largely possess 3D structural geometry with numerous topologies and strong electron migration within the backbone, it is challenging to synthesize 3D COFs that adopt high electronic conductivity. These disadvantages in synthesizing predesigned COFs hinder their possibilities in many applications, in which diverse structures are required. No 3D COFs for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has been reported and we recognize that there are many urgent tasks that must be studied (e.g., employing diverse approaches to synthesize COFs with suitable electronic structures; improving yield and selectivity of products as well as durability of COF catalysts) to enhance the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photocatalytic performance of COFs.
