## 6. Conclusion and Outlook
The chemistry of reticular materials holds great promise for the construction of fascinating structures, providing unlimited powerful tools for the structural functionalization. Therefore, a variety of applications have been developed, of which the utilization of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is of significant interest to mitigate the negatively impact on the global warming and greenhouse phenomenon. Reticular materials, including MOFs and COFs, offer undoubtedly tremendous characteristics of porous crystalline compounds to convert $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into higher value-added compounds. This is one of the key factors in solving global energetic problems.
In this Report, we concisely overview the fundamental of the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . This article especially outlines from the basic knowledge of the photoreduction to the mechanisms and methods for gaining a deep comprehension of the process of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. Based on these discussion, we selectively highlight studies that reported 1) the structural design of reticular materials adopting semiconducting features, 2) the structural functionalization of MOFs/COFs for the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and 3) the strategic approaches for creating MOF/COF photocatalysts suited to the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under various conditions. The intellectual discussion is given with the relationship between structural features of reticular materials and their photocatalytic properties, thus rendering a profound knowledge for scientists this field to design the effectively catalytic systems for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. Additionally, we propose several approaches for future directions comprehensively discussed below.
## 6.1. MOFs Based on Lewis Active Sites
While the high conjugation linkers have been intensively studied due to their electronic properties in enhancing the separation of the photogenerated charges, the role of active metal sites are not commonly recognized for the designed synthesis of MOF photocatalysts. The high Lewis acidity of metal-oxo clusters in MOFs has been largely explored in a variety of applications, e.g., from gas storage to catalysis and sensing.[156–159] Although the exceptional stability of zeolites or metal oxides highlights their industrially applicable level for years, their catalytic performance remains insignificant due to the weak Lewis acidity.[25] MOFs with well-defined structures consisting of strong Lewis acidic centers have been proven to be effective catalysts in various organic transformations (may replace zeolites to some extent) potentially inspiring scientists to seek for diverse catalyst structures suited to the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Metal SBUs with strong Lewis acidic centers can be incorporated into MOFs using 1) in situ formation of OMS (Cu, Fe, Co, Cr, Al, etc.), 2) defect clusters obtained via the controlled concentration of modulators, and 3) PSM approach, in which metal exchanges have been the common methods.[25] This suggestion has been evidenced by the reported researches that presented the high Lewis acidity of Fe-oxo clusters in MIL series,[80] $Z\mathrm{r}$ and Hf-based MOFs,[160] and mixed ${\mathrm{Zr/Ti}}{\mathrm{.MOF}}$ materials.[96] Very recently, Liu and coworkers have published their research indicating the importance of reticulation of mixed $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}{-}\mathrm{M}$ $(\mathrm{M}~=~\mathrm{Co}$ , Ni, Zn) clusters into 3D structures, NNU-31-M, capable of reducing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to HCOOH using neither photosensitizer nor electron donor agent with $100\%$ selectivity.[161] This work powerfully highlights the potential of using influential Lewis acidic sites in combination with the suitable organic linkers in promoting more value-added products from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
Nevertheless, it is imperative to solve the bottleneck of MOFs based on the strong Lewis acidic centers (normally MOFs with OMS) that are low water stability.[162] The open question for emerging scholars is to find the optimum approaches to synthesize MOF photocatalysts whose features combine all 1) excellent stability under harsh conditions, i.e., the long durability of catalysts for the photocatalytic process is urgently required, 2) large surface area and high accessible interaction sites, and 3) suitable bandgap energy for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in one.
## 6.2. Defects
The chemistry of defects has been of great significant interest and applied in many applications due to its powerful capability of tailoring the structural aspects of MOFs.[163,164] It has been intensively studied for the last decade. Especially, defected MOFs have been applied to gas storage and separation, luminescent materials, and catalytic platforms.[164] It is explainable that defects not only offer surface chemistry modification but also adjust pore structures which positively alter 1) the sorption properties (i.e., increasing adsorption affinity) and 2) the Lewis acidity of MOFs, thus improving their photocatalytic performance. Nevertheless, little data relevant to the interplay between defects and photocatalytic properties in MOFs have reported, although defects play important roles in zeolites[165] and semiconductors for organic synthesis and the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , respectively.[166] In the critical review reported by Fang et  al., the authors firmly stated, “the incorporation of defects further offers the possibility to fine-tune bandgaps for optical applications.”[164]
Based on the classification of defects, the catalytic features of MOFs that consist of cluster defects, linker vacancies, and/ or OMS Lewis acidic defects will potentially be investigated. For example, in MOF composites section, the role of oxygen defects in $\mathrm{znO}$ component of heterojunction-based MOFs[129] in stabilizing the intermediates and facilitating the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photo­ reduction was discussed in detail, proving that there exist vacant rooms with various opportunities for exploring the applications of defected MOFs in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. Very recently, Gate and coworkers have reported the advanced chemistry of defected UiO-66 for many kinds of organic transformations.[167,168] These studies highlight the applicability of defected MOFs in catalysis; researches that deal with defects for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are highly encouraged.
## 6.3. Single-Crystal-to-Single-Crystal Exchange
The reticular community has been focusing much on diversifying MOF structures leading to the majority achievement.[169] This is time for us to carry out studies that provide a deeper understanding of the mechanism and beyond. We are in a position of finding effective MOFs for producing methanol, methane, even hydrogen carbons from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . It is apparent that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has been largely converted into CO or formic acid with a two-electron pathway; there are many competitive and challenging tasks to convert $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into valuable carbon-neutral products with multiple-electron reduction pathways. Toward this end, we wish to highlight researches that utilized photonic crystals of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ,[170] Au $\mathsf{N P S}^{[171]}$ photocatalyzing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to form methane, methane and ethane, respectively. In the chemistry of MOFs based on group IV (Ti, $\operatorname{Zr},$ and Hf), using modulators that reduce the speed of crystallization give rise to large single crystals; $[172-174]$ employing the mixed metal strategy $(\mathrm{Ti}/\mathrm{Zr})$ has been applied for altering the energy gaps of mixed metal MOFs.[65,96] However, literature has no studies that report the effects of large single crystals of Ti-MOFs[175] or ${\mathrm{Ti}}/{\mathrm{Zr}}{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ (both can be synthesized by in situ formations or single-crystal-to-single-crystal exchange method) in promoting the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. This approach belongs to the future direction of research.
## 6.4. Hierarchical Structures
Scientists believe that the hierarchical systems of nature (woods, cells, bones, polymers, and many more) are intentionally made up of components that display exceptional properties enabling the natural progress (photosynthesis, metabolites).[176] It is merely due to their hierarchical levels of structures. In reticular chemistry, this concept existed and is widely accepted as “heterogeneity” which includes the formation of structures based on mixed linkers, mixed metals, and/or high level of sophisticated components.[177] There is a report recently published by Chen group using a hierarchical MOF for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation.[178] At the same time, Ma group reported the efficiency of hierarchical COF in enhancing the enzyme accessibility and catalytic activity.[179] It is worth noting that there is very little data showing the exceptional properties of hierarchical structures of MOFs or COFs capable of reducing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under photocatalytic conditions. This task challenges scientists in creating the complex structures with targeted features (e.g., suitable bandgap energy, strong interaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and high durability) for the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
## 6.5. Photoelectrochemical Reaction (PEC)
Thanks to the pioneering works by Honda and Halman in the early stage of the photoelectrochemistry,[11,180] scientists are recently more prone to use the PEC system. The PEC consists of three components that are working electrode (WE), counter electrode (CE), and reference electrode (RE) simultaneously working in a cell with an electrolyte. The working principle of this PEC system is described as follow. Under sunlight or the visible light irradiation, the WE containing a semiconductor/photocatalyst absorbs the photon energy generating the photogenerated electrons and holes. The half-cell reaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ will be taking place in this WE. If the semiconductor is $n$ -type, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction occurs on the photoanode vice versa if the semiconductor is p-type, the WE is photocathode where the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction takes place. The other half-cell oxidation reaction occurs on the CE. The WE and CE are separated by a proton exchange membrane only permitting $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ to pass by to reach the WE. Therefore, hydrogen production is always competitive with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. The advantage of PEC is that products of hall-cell reactions will separately be harvested, thus enhancing the production rate because the products of hall-cell reduction cannot be re-oxidized. Moreover, the efficiency of the photoreduction using PEC can straightforwardly be controlled by applying external bias providing more electrons for the reduction reaction. Therefore, it is recommended to use MOFs/ COFs fabricated onto the WC of the PEC system. Nonetheless, there are few drawbacks of this method which are the electrical energy consumption and the intricate handling of the PEC system (i.e., it is more complicated than the photochemical reaction, in which the reaction proceeds within a homogeneous medium).[16]
